Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to isolate Spain.
In response to the government’s sudden decision to reimpose quarantine measures on Spain, Dominic Raab offered an explanation.
The ministers announced on Saturday that travelers who fail to return from Spain and its islands before midnight will be required to self-isolate for 14 days.
Spain continues to be on the list of “travel corridors” for safe travel established by No. 10 Downing Street, but recent spikes in coronavirus cases have prompted public concerns about a second wave.
Sunday, Foreign Secretary made an explanation for the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions measures, saying that it was a “real-time reaction” to the surge in reported infections in the region.
He said that he had assessed the situation the previous afternoon and “we made our decision quickly.”
He added, “We cannot apologize for this.”
"We must be able to take rapid and decisive action, especially in cases involving our own country or Spain and specific countries, we must act."
Otherwise, Britain would face another risk of infection and trigger a second wave of the epidemic, followed by another lockdown.
The transport minister faces mandatory quarantine like those going on holiday to Spain.
After two consecutive days with over 900 new daily cases reported in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists had already missed their chance to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said, "I know that for those in Spain or considering going to Spain, this decision is very unkind; but we must be able to take rapid and decisive action to protect Britain."
“In reducing the number of cases and combating the resurgence of the epidemic, we have made some notable progress.”
Shadow Health Secretary Jonathan Ashworth condemns the government’s approach as “completely haphazard.”
He said that Downing Street’s sudden decision left holidaymakers “both bewildered and disappointed.”
The Labour MP told Sophie Ridge that I understand why they made this decision, but it was done without any sense of proportion in the last 24 hours.
“The vacationer in Spain and those planning to visit Tenerife or other islands will be confused and frustrated that employers will not accept two weeks of quarantine.”
"The government is just saying 'we wish for employer cooperation.'"
"They made this decision without any sense in the last 24 hours."
CDC says that Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne illness that causes an estimated 135 million infections annually, hospitalizations for 26,500 people, and deaths in 420 individuals.
Common symptoms of salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever, and abdominal cramps, which may appear within six to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last four to seven days and most people recover without treatment; however, some cases require hospitalization.
A couple in California’s dog park was attacked during lunchtime because they were not wearing masks (video) - RT TV's US News
First comes face mask insults, then fists and kicks.
A couple from California was confronted with this situation when they were having lunch together at a dog park with their dogs. A woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they weren’t wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said they went to lunch at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Boston terrier on Thursday.
When two spouses took off their masks and prepared to eat, an “elderly woman who suddenly appeared out of nowhere” walked up to them and called them “idiots” because they were not wearing masks, although they had said that eating with a mask was impossible.
O'Brien sends the woman away from the park and stops her harassment behavior at that moment, when she sprays pepper spray at both of them.
The video that he captured was taken just as Kelly and the girl were fighting over the canister; he was sprayed in the face.
After the attackers leave with their dogs, O'Brien can be heard sobbing in the background.
O'Brien uploaded the video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he wanted this woman “to go to prison.”
California mandates masks statewide, even in fresh air places.
People can remove their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
But, the state’s coronavirus pandemic response guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to urge compliance with this rule.
Even in this “mask shame” era, it is still a crime and will be punished with a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
The boy from the Edo town who sold eggs refused to give bribes and was overturned; he received an apartment and free education of 100 rupees in exchange.
It is reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar’s cart filled with eggs was overturned by municipal officials in Pardeshi.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who couldn’t sell eggs and whose cart was overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, he received support from people all over China to help his family recover losses and restart business.
The boy’s family also said that politicians including Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had contacted them for help.
The boy's family also received a set of apartments and an undertaking to cover their children's education costs.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar’s small cart selling eggs had its wheels knocked off by municipality officials of Mardol as the city implemented “from left to right” staggered opening measures in the wake of the pandemic.
In a video, a boy says that officials warned him this morning that if he did not pay 100 rupees in bribes, they would overturn his car. The clip quickly spread on social media.
The boy said that after he refused to hand over 100 rupees, those officials overturned his little car and smashed all the eggs.
The boy still says in the video that due to the impact of the epidemic, daily sales of eggs have dropped, and losses from these eggs will be an added burden for him.
Since Thursday, the boy’s family has received a large number of donations from all over the country, including cash and kind-hearted assistance.
The locals also offered assistance to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh has provided funding and helped children get into an institution; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also guarantees that under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme, his family will receive a house.
The Indoors Club also provided rations and money to boys.
The offices of National Conference leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also came to his aid.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people all over the country."
Mr. Ramesh Mendola of the People's Party guarantees that we will be provided with an apartment under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and an installment of Rs. 2,500.
Digvijaya Singh then provided Rs. 10,000 and promised to take care of two children’s education.
Rahul Gandhi's aides also called to help us.
A female staffer representing Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal from Delhi also called us to assist us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also supported her.
Home Secretary Priti Patel demands an explanation from Twitter and Instagram for inappropriate content released by Wiley
Because Twitter and Instagram failed to promptly remove inappropriate content published by Wiley, Priti Patel is demanding an explanation from both platforms.
The police are now investigating a series of anti-Semitic remarks made by this Grime artist on social media.
Minister of Internal Affairs Sunday tweeted that “the anti-Semitic content published by Wiley is extremely alarming.”
"Twitter and Instagram did not promptly delete these contents; I have asked them to provide adequate explanations."
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.
He criticized Twitter for ignoring antisemitism because of Wiley’s tweet that was still visible twelve hours after its release.
The website subsequently deleted multiple tweets and suspended the artist’s account for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily closed @Wiley’s account because of violations of our hate speech policy; Facebook said Instagram does not allow for hateful content.
Saturday, the Metropolitan Police Department said that it was “extremely concerned” about all reports of anti-Semitism and is assessing relevant materials.
Meanwhile, A-List, Wiley’s PR firm, has severed ties with this star.
The singer’s previous agent, John Woolf, who is also Jewish, wrote on Twitter, “Given Wiley’s recent anti-Semitic content, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all our ties with him.” This society has no place for anti-Semitism.
Earlier he had indicated that he would neither support nor condone Wiley’s comments, but would privately communicate with him and “educate” him.
However, an Instagram video shows that Wiley appears to have severed ties with his management company.
Other legislators also criticized Twitter for not promptly deleting the posts.
Lib Dem MP Jess Phillips said on Saturday that she had just seen what Wiley was up to. Why did @Twitter allow such blatant anti-Semitism and hate content to remain? It involved many dangerous ideas: Jews get things you don’t, they control everything, they think they’re better than us, etc. Such content is very dangerous and should be deleted.
Member Neil Coyle adds that his agent seems to react faster than @Twitter, and I reiterate that we should ensure social media platforms do not become vehicles for spreading hate through legislation (including the Online Harms Bill).
Government anti-Semitism independent advisor John Mann said these posts “precisely reflect why changes to the law are needed to properly hold those responsible.”
The anti-Semitic campaign also requires police to investigate Wiley’s content and call for his social media accounts to be closed “to prevent another spurt of anti-Semitic venom.”
Wiley's real name is Richard Cowie and he has been called "The Father of Grime" for his contributions to music. He was awarded an MBE in recognition of his work in the field in 2018.
Kudlow says next coronavirus relief package would provide more stimulus checks and extend eviction moratorium
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow on Sunday pledged that the federal government will extend the moratorium on evictions and issue another round of stimulus checks under the next coronavirus relief package.
Just weeks before Kudlow made his promise, the White House payroll tax cut was rejected by Congress.
The residents of Massart Mallawi village did not wish to comment on this matter and merely expressed condolences to the families of the children involved.
Khalid Darwish, an advisor to the National Council for Women and Children, said in October that about 93 percent of Egyptian children aged one to fourteen had been victims of violence.
He also noted that litigation is one of the major tools for eliminating child abuse and emphasizes that cooperation between the Committee and the prosecuting authorities in this regard must be enhanced.
A study by the National Committee on Women and Children found that children may be victimized even in places they should have been safest – at home, school, or club – where perpetrators include people who were supposed to protect them, such as parents or teachers.
The Associated Press criticized White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany’s “political briefing,” but journalists continue to ask political questions on RT America.
The AP accuses White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany of making news more political than newsworthy, causing concern among journalists but apparently forgetting that reporters choose what to ask.
The Associated Press, America’s largest news institution, published an article on Saturday that suggests that press conferences at the White House might “break the mold” because McEnany will turn them into “a short and overtly aggressive place to promote or attack media as part of campaigning for re-election,” while information about what the government has been doing is “relatively sparse.”
The last review is not without merit because there was little to no new information disclosed at the press conference.
The Associated Press did not mention that few people asked any new information at press conferences.
Journalists often use press secretaries at the White House to make themselves noticed, as CNN’s Jim Acosta has done to his advantage.
They transcend the common adversarial relationship between media and government, repeating questions that have traps for questioning President Donald Trump.
In some cases, they will make themselves news, for example, when CBS journalist Weijia Jiang responded to Trump’s “ask China back,” she made an unflattering suggestion: "Mr., why did you say this to me?"
The launch on July 6 was a good example of that fact, showing it is no longer the source for effective information in American publics.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR’s ratings had dropped because of false reporting on so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and his decision to ban display of Confederate flags in races.
As expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to that tweet, seeking her personal confirmation of Trump’s support for the confederate flag.
McEnany did not let reporters get away with it; such an interpretation would damage Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the confederate flag and that his tweets were about NASCAR fans being wrongly portrayed as racists.
The Associated Press reports that she sounded “insulted” during the press conference.
The Associated Press story resonated with remarks published earlier in the month on the front page of The Washington Post by journalist Jonathan Karl, also head of the White House Correspondents Association.
Karl says that denying reality and using the White House podium for purely political purposes is contrary to public trust.
McEnany took over as press secretary in April and chaired her first news conference on May 1st, with her intelligence and tenacity attracting some Trump supporters to regard her as an heroine.
The news director position had been vacant for more than a year before she was appointed.
Like other aspects of Washington politics under Trump’s administration, the relationship between the White House press secretary and the media has changed dramatically.
Such changes have come about from both sides before McEnany's arrival.
In April 2018 at a White House press briefing, comedian Michelle Wolf viciously interrogated former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders, and even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it “shocking.”
Jill Colvin, of Associated Press at the first White House press conference in McEnany, asked her if she could pledge that “you will never lie on that stage for us.”
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume is a seasoned news person with more than 50 years of experience who served as ABC’s chief White House press secretary from 1989 to 1996 and said that today’s journalists exhibit “aggressive behavior” in news conferences because they don’t respect Trump.
He said on this week’s Tucker Carlson Tonight on Fox News that this was because reporters focused on countering Trump rather than collecting policy news.
“It is not just a game of cat and mouse,” Hume said in reference to media people’s stated objective.
Our goal is not merely to get government officials off the stage but to obtain information.
California protesters burn courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police say that on Saturday evening, a peaceful protest in California turned violent when protesters set fire to court buildings and smashed police stations before attacking several officers.
Local authorities in Auckland tweeted that protesters damaged windows, sprayed graffiti, and fired fireworks and used laser pointers at police officers.
Some have called for peace on Twitter, as seen in tweets that read: “We request organizers to help us provide safe spaces and locations for the demonstrators.”
Protests began Saturday evening and were led by organizations like “Mamawall” (which is similar to an organization in Portland, Oregon), among others. Meanwhile, protesters clashed with security personnel deployed at the federal courthouse to provide security for the trial.
Since May 25th in Minneapolis, when George Floyd died under police custody, President Donald Trump has dispatched federal police to suppress nightly protests.
At first, peaceful demonstrations took place in Auckland, but transformed into violent clashes at night.
Around midnight, police announced that this demonstration was an illegal assembly and demanded the protesters to disperse.
According to news reports, there was an arson incident at the Alameda County Superior Court, but it was brought under control within a short time.
The police said that at one point, protesters smashed windows and made racially charged slurs against residents.
Departments related to the incident posted photos on Twitter showing that the police station’s glass had been smashed and graffiti was scrawled all over the walls.
United Nations High Commissioner cancelled visit to Pakistan due to flight issues
Islamabad (AP) – UN General Assembly President-elect announced on Sunday that she had postponed her scheduled visit to Pakistan due to technical problems with flights.
In May last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was elected as the President of the General Assembly for its 75th session, which was scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that plans to visit the capital of Pakistan had been postponed.
Pakistan’s Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweets in response to Bozkir expressing his “warm welcome for you to visit Pakistan as soon as possible and conduct an engaging and constructive visit.”
Just one day ago, Qureshi announced his visit with great glee, expressing his desire to discuss a range of issues, including Kashmir, which lies between Pakistan and its neighboring India, both of which claim sovereignty over the entire region.
Since gaining independence from British rule in 1947, Pakistan and India have fought three wars, of which two involved that disputed region.
The two sides have frequently engaged in firefights in Kashmir, resulting in casualties among military personnel and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people in the Himalayan region to decide whether they want to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for the settlement of the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United qualifies for Champions League, Paul Pogba hints at staying in the team
After Red Bull has secured its fourth-place finish, Manchester United star Paul Pogba hinted that he will remain with the club this summer.
2-0 victory over Leicester means that Manchester United will finish third and ahead of Frank Lampard's Chelsea team.
Leicester City still led Manchester United by 14 points going into the first round of matches but Red Devils stormed through to reach the semi-finals.
In the last two years, Pogba has twice asked to leave the club and so fans have predicted that he will depart this summer.
But the pandemic means few teams will spend heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both transfer fees and wages to bring Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Such an explanation has been helpful in recent weeks as his performance and body language have improved.
After the team won 2-0, this French player posted on Instagram to suggest he would stay with the club.
While Pogba may merely be celebrating his team’s return to UEFA Champions League qualification, many fans read it as an emphatic indication that he will remain in the squad and duke it out with European elite players.
Pogba’s current contract is for one year, but the club can extend it another year, meaning he will actually be with the team for two years.
Manchester United is keen to extend Pogba’s contract and wants to clarify his situation before the next season.
For Pogba, staying with the team is comfortable, and his arrival of Bruno Fernandes has made him stronger.
This Portuguese midfielder now forms part of the heart of Old Trafford with his arrival allowing Pogba to push deeper into midfield and improve his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to Premier League relegation.
Some of Watford’s players knelt down and others left the pitch without turning around.
The whistle of Watford’s return to English football after five seasons has just blown, but no one can say that in a time when Watford must chase Arsenal with all its strength, it did not fight until defeat was inevitable.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and incisive performance in five years, as they were close to reversing the game when trailing 3-0 before succumbing to Arsenal.
With just 16 minutes to go in the match, Danny Welbeck's backheel volley was saved by goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez, and had it not been for that save, Welbeck would have earned his third point for Watford, level with Arsenal and secure their place in the Premier League.
But ultimately, Watford failed to get what it wanted, as its caretaker coach Hayden Mullins said that if his team had played like they did in this game, he would not have been so tireless and laborious.
The entire season, Watford has been lacklustre and boss Pozzo’s management of the club is also poor.
A failed battle may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzos have no chance of laying down a foundation that would ensure Waltham Forest’s return to the Premier League.
Watford's frequent managerial changes have become an inescapable fate.
In sum, it will be eight years before another change of leadership occurs, but this time around there is little new blood to inject.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with his team and will still be able to play in the UAE Super League.
Some players may also choose to leave, for example, striker Ismaïla Sarr has again said that it is almost unbelievable to play in League One in September, but saving strength remains the top priority at this stage.
Arsenal’s manager Mikel Arteta says that the odds of two teams meeting up are hard to predict, making the game more exciting.
Arteta also said that Watford's strikers were “cheating”, and soon changed his mind to the footballer's term for “trying to stay in play”.
Arsenal’s statement was entirely justified, for Arsenal is ahead, so Watford must press relentlessly to narrow the deficit.
Watford conceded a penalty in the opening minutes.
At the start of the match, Craig Dawson accidentally knocked Alexandre Lacazette over, which was subsequently ruled as an offside by the VAR for about three minutes before being awarded a penalty kick, which Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang converted.
Watford pressed hard, Sarr quickly counterattacked with a right-side ball, Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover to push up toward the goalmouth.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that they would still sue even if Corbyn were expelled from the party by Starmer.
What I mean is that he really made the Labor Party competitive again.
He still has a long way to go in terms of policy, but he already made an impression that is very strong; he has succeeded so much that many former Labor Party supporters who were not Labour voters anymore have now re-affirmed their support for Labor and view him as a serious and earnest figure.
Blair really does worry that Labour is still seen as just a tax party.
He said, "According to my experience, if you're a member of the Labor Party, then in tax matters, you have to be very careful because the public is not sitting there worrying whether or not you’re going to raise taxes; they are more likely worried that you will raise taxes, so you need to pay attention to this mindset."
The Coronavirus Will Forever Change the World Economy (Opinion)
Just as security measures proliferate after 9/11, virus screening could become part of our lives.
It is imperative to make necessary investments in infrastructure to prepare for future virus testing.
If coronavirus immunity has short-term effects, these investments can provide a safety net for economic operation.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies akin to Germany’s “short-time work” (Kurzarbeit) subsidy.
Under this policy, working hours and wages will be reduced accordingly, with any shortfall in wages to be subsidized by the government.
This initiative maintains the link between enterprises and workers, laying a solid foundation for rapid economic recovery.
Improving policy implementation is also important; these policies need to become permanent components of economic recovery tools.
Remote work is likely to become more common.
There is evidence to suggest that remote work can be as effective as in-office work.
However, many companies remain reluctant to adopt remote-working arrangements.
Given that remote work has been widely tried and is showing good results, it may well continue.
With e-commerce further developing, remote healthcare, video conferencing, online teaching, and financial technology have seen increasing adoption, with the pandemic crisis accelerating digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are struggling with shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many of these companies move production back to their home countries.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop said that the pandemic had “devastating” impacts on all activities.
Restrictions put in place to combat the spread of the coronavirus have affected events such as festivals and highland games in Scotland.
Ms. Hyslop said that while we have taken some positive steps, such as the resumption of car movie theaters, many industries will not be able to operate for an extended period.
We have engaged in close and constructive cooperation with industry organizers to develop creative solutions for addressing our respective challenges in safe operations.
A portion of the funding will also be used to promote innovation and support the long-term recovery of the events.
The Event Industry Advisory Group believes that this industry is one of the first to be restricted and will also be one of the last to be fully lifted.
The chairman of the group, Peter Duthie, also holds an executive position at the Glasgow Stock Exchange, where he said that events generate around £5 billion in revenue for Scotland each year.
He said that it was necessary for governments to provide additional funding to this industry, which had gained widespread support from event organizers and many related supply chain enterprises in their long-term lockdown restrictions, all of whom suffered serious economic losses.
Shivraj Singh Chouhan is confirmed to have contracted coronavirus.
Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh, Shivraj Singh Chouhan, confirmed Saturday that his coronavirus test results were positive and said in a tweet posted Sunday morning that he was “fine” and praised those on the front lines of the fight against the pandemic.
The chief minister reiterated his warnings from yesterday and called on everyone to abide by the guidelines for preventing the spread of disease, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
“Friends, I’m fine. The heroes who fought against the pandemic risked their lives and gave freely without any expectation of reward; they all deserve praise. I salute all the frontline workers in Wuhan, China.” Chouhan tweeted from the Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal yesterday when he was admitted to the hospital.
“Maintain two-meter distance, wash your hands frequently, and wear masks – these are our most effective weapons in fighting against coronavirus,” he said, hoping that everyone could use them to protect themselves and their families.
Chouhan added, “Be careful all of you, and I hope that all of you will be safe and healthy as well. This is also what I pray for to God.”
Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that he had tested positive for the virus despite his best efforts to avoid such an occurrence; he warned that even a little bit of carelessness could lead to infection with the coronavirus.
Today morning, Chouhan tweeted that while there is still a threat of infection, people “don’t need to be afraid” and urged those with symptoms to get tested.
He said, "If you have contracted the virus, there is no need to fear. Do not conceal your symptoms; immediately inform the doctor and start treatment. Timely treatment will help you recover."
Central states have reported nearly 27,000 cases of confirmed infection, with 760 active cases and 799 deaths related to the coronavirus.
In recent weeks, India has seen an increase in confirmed cases, with daily new infections exceeding 45,000 since July 23rd.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 has rapidly approached 1.4 million; over 467,000 were active cases and more than 32,000 deaths have been attributed to the virus.
So what would be more suitable for studying rocks on Mars than one from Mars? Prof. Caroline Smith, curator of meteorites at the Natural History Museum, puts it like this.
Smith is also a member of the “Mars 2020” science team and adds that scientists believe the rocks they returned were from Mars.
The tiny bubbles in the meteorite match exactly with those of Mars’ atmosphere, so we can be certain that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this meteorite was formed during a collision between Mars and a small asteroid or comet around six to seventy thousand years ago, accompanied by fragments scattered in space.
One rock was swept through the solar system and landed on Earth.
This meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been conserved at the Natural History Museum ever since.
Among the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover is a high-precision laser spectrometer called Sherloc that will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic molecules capable of indicating past or present life on Mars.
Adding a SAU 008 is to ensure maximum precision for this operation.
Smith adds: “We specifically selected this rock because it matched its composition chemically and was also very hard.”
"We have discovered some Martian meteorites that are quite fragile; this one is as hard as an old boot."
Upon locating valuable rocks of research interest, "Perseverance" will store them on the Martian surface.
Then these rocks will be sent to Mars robots for recovery and then launched from space to Earth for research analysis.
Mambourin near Villebien discovered a man wounded by gunfire.
Last night, a man shot in the west of Melbourne was rushed to hospital for emergency treatment.
After receiving an alarm, police arrived at nearby Mambourin in Verrières, around 8 p.m., where they found a man of about 20 years old with suspected multiple gunshot wounds on his lower half.
A man was sent to the hospital for treatment and is in stable condition.
The police said they were investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays and just wear my pajamas."
Question: How did your Sunday start?
I stumbled down the stairs on my way to make tea for my wife and was knocked over by a dog.
After disembarking from the ferry, we drove up to the A3 motorway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of our workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed while I came back after walking my dog and had another bowl of cereal.
I am happy to return to London from White Island because I know that if I do not do this, there would be no home for me on White Island.
Kenyan police officer arrested over gun attack in Garissa
Two Kenyan policemen have been arrested on suspicion of killing two people in a shooting incident.
The shooting incident occurred in Saturday's market of Soko Ng'ombe, Eastern Kasarana County, where protests were taking place against a case involving a murder suspect.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenya Police Inspectorate has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The investigation includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Gladysa MP Aden Duale calls it “shocking” and condemns police brutality in a post on Facebook.
Several activists also expressed concern about police conduct during the pandemic.
According to Amnesty International data, since March, Kenyan police have killed at least 21 people for not complying with curfew and mask-wearing orders during the pandemic.
In early January, police used tear gas and arrested several participants in the annual Saba Saba protest – an event that originated from a 30-year-old anti-dictatorial protest against former President Daniel arap Moi – held in Nairobi.
The front-line reporter said that the protests this year were more intense because of police repression during the pandemic lockdown.
Security measures have been heightened at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China.
Sunday, security at the US Consulate General in Chengdu, China, was stepped up as American personnel prepared to depart.
Three moving trucks enter the museum; police in uniforms and plainclothes officers stand on either side of the street, with metal fences set up along the roadside.
China’s Global Times editor posted on Twitter that US Consulate in Chengdu was asked to close within 72 hours, i.e., Monday, July 27th at 10am (GMT 0200).
China accuses some personnel of the US consulate in Chengdu of “engaging in activities inconsistent with their identities.”
China’s Foreign Ministry spokesperson Wang Wenbin did not offer any details and simply stated that such activities interfere with China’s internal affairs and undermine its security interests.
China requested on Friday that the US close its Consulate General in Chengdu, which is a countermeasure to the US demand for the closure of China's Consulate General in Houston, Texas.
Washington wants China to close its Consulate General in Houston "to protect US intellectual property and private information of Americans".
After the US Embassy in Houston closed down on Friday, a group of people who looked like American officials broke through the back door to enter the place.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this behavior seriously violated international law and bilateral agreements and China will respond, but did not specify any measures.
It further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage as well as human rights issues by closing embassies in a confrontational manner.
The pranksters provide wrecking tools, and in the zoo, baboons wave all sorts of implements.
The primates of the Nossil Wildlife Park in Montgomeryshire have earned their reputation – they will charge at cars stopped within the fences and break up the wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Someone saw baboons at North Shire Wildlife Park in Merseyside using knives, scissors and chainsaws.
Staffers fear that pranksters will provide these annoying primates with weapons and they will pounce on stopped cars, smashing windshield wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Now, staff fear that tourists will use “jokingly” to provide lethal weapons for them, making them more aggressive and even seeing these baboons rummaging through toolboxes.
A park worker told The Sunday Times that “we’re not sure whether it was tourists who provided them with weapons to attack cars or they stole from pickup trucks and cargo vans.”
They do indeed go through other people's toolboxes and take them with them everywhere. Someone has seen baboons dragging chainsaws.
Another staff member said, "We have seen baboons carrying knives and screwdrivers. I wonder if it's the tourists who give them these."
Reports of baboons attacking have been seen, even in North West England, where a handyman knows this zoo.
A worker from Manchester Zoo in Greater Manchester said that people knew what was going to happen at the zoo this year and his two clients were mauled by baboons.
They further said that just one minute before the children wanted monkeys to sit on top of the car and disappear the next minute when the license plate was missing. This kind of problem brought good business to repair shops.
Nossier Zoo insists that it is as safe here as McDonald's drive-through and will reopen on June 15 after taking precautionary measures to address the coronavirus pandemic, even though visitors can stay in their vehicles for the entire five-mile wildlife park tour.
The zoo occupies an area of about 222.7 hectares and houses lions, tigers, rhinos, wildebeest, and camels among others; they scoff at reports that baboons carry weapons.
A spokesman said that “we believe many of these stories have been exaggerated through word-of-mouth and have signs of embellishment, which sometimes makes it hard to believe what is found in the park.”
In 2012, Hyundai Motors placed its new i30 car in this zoo for ten hours to prove that the model was strong enough to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea advance to the Champions League; Barnsley and Watford fall out of the Premier League.
Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea won against Leicester City at the end of the season in the Premier League to qualify for the Champions League while Barnsley and Watford were relegated after five years of dominance.
Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard’s goals put Manchester United ahead in the game with a 2-0 victory over Leicester City, while Ole Gunnar Solskjaer's team secured third place.
Chelsea’s goals and assists for Mason Mount secured a 2-0 win over Wolves, moving up to fourth place in the table and securing their last Europa League spot ahead of Leicester City (who were generally in the top four during the season).
Leicester City will also participate in the European League, and so will Tottenham, which drew with Wolves at Crystal Palace, but because it had more goals scored than Wolves.
To avoid the last-minute clash for the remaining two promotion spots, Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute and equalized one minute later with a goal from the substitute, leaving them tied at 1-1 with West Ham United.
Jack Grealish scores for Aston Villa and it might be his last game for his childhood club.
Bournemouth won 3-1 against Everton but were one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford, having lost to Arsenal 3-2, was relegated as the bottom team.
Manchester United scored more than 100 goals during the season and won with a 5-0 score against the relegated Norwich City, securing their place in the Champions League. This was David Silva's final game for Manchester United after 10 years of service.
Kevin De Bruyne breaks records with 20 assists and two goals in the season.
Liverpool defeated Newcastle United 3-1 to end their title quest for this season and finished with 99 points.
In addition, Southampton won 3-1 against Sheffield United and Danny Ings has scored 22 goals this season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy has scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes that her children will develop good values.
Gwyneth Paltrow says she tries to educate her children with “not just giving fish,” hoping they will develop good values.
Actress Jennifer Lawrence has two children with her ex-husband, Chris Martin, including 16-year-old Apple and 14-year-old Moses. She hopes to instill good values in them rather than relying on his fame.
“I also think it’s important to cultivate children’s professional ethics because… I believe that having their values aligned with your own or with how they see themselves is very important,” she told Rob Lowe on the podcast.
“Reality is that I can point you in the right direction, but if you just try like everyone else, you won’t get anywhere.”
She adds: "As parents, I can do nothing more for my children than to teach them right from wrong and what work is." I have tried hard not just to give them fish.
Portman also took steps to ensure that children’s lives were kept as private as possible and their social media accounts remained private.
Given that there will be new elections, the Renewal Party eventually agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz accuses Pelosi of wanting "to solve problems with money and to shut down America."
WASHINGTON – Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz accuses House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and her Democratic colleagues of pushing an economic recovery package that he says is “paying for problems with money and shutting down America.” The follow-up negotiations continue.
Cruz said on Sunday that the US faces two crises simultaneously: a global pandemic that has taken over 600, 000 lives worldwide and an “absolute economic disaster” in which America finds itself.
Cruz criticized Pelosi for speaking before he had been interviewed about any of those issues.
Cruz says her goal is to use money to solve problems and bring down the US government.
"You especially have to look at her push for the $3 trillion bill, which is purely about throwing money around rather than solving any real problems. Our goal should be getting Americans back to work so they can take care of their families."
The Senate Republican Party and the White House last week discussed a fourth-stage plan for dealing with the pandemic, which is likely to amount to $1 trillion.
Last weekend, White House Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill for further discussions with Senate staff members, where Mnuchin told Fox News on Sunday that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell will reveal the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue for lawmakers and the focus of debate between Republicans and Democrats is that the additional unemployment benefits have expired and will be terminated this weekend.
The Democrats want to retain an additional $600 in aid for “Phase IV,” while Republicans call for its reduction, arguing that some Americans earn more from unemployment benefits than they do from wages.
Cruz says that half of American workers now receive more unemployment benefits than they earn while working, and he notes that some small business owners in Texas have had trouble hiring because of it.
He said, “They certainly won’t come back to work because the federal government pays them sometimes more than twice their wages.”
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill to ease taxes and regulations for small businesses so that Americans can return to work.
He said: “The recovery bill is not to throw millions of dollars at it; rather, it suspends payroll taxes, which means that every working American gets a raise. This is how we can get people back to work.”
President Trump last week asked for a new bailout package to include cuts in payroll taxes, but Mnuchin said that proposal would not be included in legislation because Republicans do not accept it.
President Trump confirmed that the next bill will not include wage cuts but is attempting to blame Democrats for it.
Cruz has raised an objection to the cost of the new plan last week and on Sunday said that he disagrees with the measure proposed by the Republican Senate.
But he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed in May by the House of Representatives controlled by Democrats for being deliberately crafted to harm the president’s interests before an election in November.
He said that “the Democrats’ sole objective is to defeat Donald Trump; they naively believe that the best way to defeat him is to shut down every aspect of American life and close every school in America.”
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns Kargil heroes and says Pakistan is trying to stab India in the back.
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Prime Minister Narendra Modi in his tribute to the martyrs of the armed forces said that while India has been trying hard to maintain friendly relations with Pakistan, it still tries to stab India from behind.
Prime Minister Modi said in his monthly radio address to the nation, "Pakistan is responsible for this disaster; they have devised sinister plans to steal Indian land and divert its domestic conflicts."
He said that “Pakistan is shameless and attempts to occupy Indian territory in order to divert attention from internal turmoil.”
At that time, India was working to repair its relations with Pakistan.
But, as it is said, the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
Such people have no good in them and will do evil even if their enemy wants to help them.
He said that this was why Pakistan did not heed India’s efforts to maintain relations and instead cut deals behind its back.
He added, “Imagine how our troops were fighting against the enemy at the top of a high mountain while their own army was below. Yet, it was our high spirits and true strength that helped us win victory.”
“The valiant armed forces have displayed our country’s great power in the Kargil War,” said Prime Minister Modi, reminiscing about what he saw and heard during his visit to the theater of the war.
India holds an annual celebration to commemorate its victory at Kargil, when the Indian army recaptured all of its posts on Ladakh Territory that had been seized by Pakistani troops over 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced that "Operation Vijay" was concluded successfully.
The fighting in Ladakh’s high-altitude snow region lasted for nearly three months and resulted in more than 500 Indian casualties.
Earlier this morning, senior officials and army leaders paid their respects to the martyrs at the Amar Jawan Jyoti monument at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of the Defence Staff Bipin Rawat, Army Chief M M Naravane, Air Chief Marshal R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambil Singh.
Relations between India and Pakistan remained tense for decades after the Kargil War, due to numerous terrorist attacks.
India insists that negotiations and terrorism cannot be simultaneous and that Pakistan must destroy all terrorist camps on its territory.
Lender firms issue warning that SME loan scheme may not achieve its goals
Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) typically do not receive much favor from large banks, so they increasingly turn to peer-to-peer lending platforms to meet their funding needs.
But Mr. Callaghan said that peer-to-peer lenders do not have an advantage over large banks because they cannot obtain the same cheap funding, and he warned that SME guarantee schemes are unpopular.
He said that I think this scheme is unlikely to achieve its purpose; people call us expecting cheap funds from us rather than half of the loan amount being provided by the government.
So they were very disappointed when we called them, because someone had expressed an interest in discounted prices, but our financing costs did not change at all.
Mr. Callaghan added that if fintech firms could obtain cheaper financing and then transfer those funds to small businesses, such a scheme would be “a piece of cake.”
"Getting in and out of this situation is a bit difficult – large banks can obtain funding at a lower cost, but they would prefer to avoid risks, so actually they don't lend funds in the same way, and unless you have collateral, they won't feel secure accessing information, " he said, "unless we can get cheaper funding, I think that people will be fantasizing about how popular such a proposal will be."
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the design of the scheme, CEO Aris Allegos said that government-suggested guarantees of 50 percent should be handed over to small businesses, but different lending institutions would take different approaches.
He says, “I understand that others may not have experienced such cost structures.”
This may relate to their capital structure or how they lend to which categories of borrowers and how they assess those risks.
Mr. Allegos declined to disclose how much lending was under the Moula scheme but said that the revised plan “concentrates on reflecting the changes that need to be made,” thereby providing greater flexibility for both borrowers and lenders.
But he said that he was very concerned about the “dual-speed economy” now in operation and its impact on small businesses.
He said that for small businesses, the biggest problems faced by Moula and loan institutions are cliffs and how to manage them.
In summary, while there has been some growth in demand, it is still not back to pre-pandemic levels.
Prospa, an Australian securities exchange-listed peer-to-peer lender, is unwilling to disclose the amount of loans it has made under the scheme, which it calls commercial sensitive information; however, CEO Greg Moshal said that the government’s extension of the scheme was well received.
He said that they supported various enhancements to this approach, which made it possible for small and medium-sized enterprises to obtain a wide range of financing products meeting their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.
Virginia Beach city demolishes the Southern Alliance monument
Virginia Beach (VA) – Sunday, Virginia Beach City Council demolished a 115-year-old Southern Alliance monument from public property. The city council unanimously approved its removal two days ago.
It was reported that workers demolished this 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was erected in 1905 outside the former Anny Princess County Court where slave auctions were held.
Thursday evening, the city council unanimously decided to undertake related demolition work.
The city council heard the views of dozens of speakers who said the monument meant division to many and reminded them constantly of painful memories.
Given recent protests and vandalism at other monuments, this one has been completely covered up, with locked gates surrounding it.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to await responses from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to deal with this monument.
Protests continue; police try to use new low-lethal tools
In Portland and across the nation, clashes between law-enforcement officials and protesters have continued, with debate raging over how to reduce police use of lethal force.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide police with new high-tech tools such as remote-controlled handcuffs and stun guns to replace pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flashbangs – weapons that are more dangerous.
The International Committee for the Prevention of Enforced Disappearances (Committee Against Enforced Disappearances) analyzed 500 videos from protests that took place between May 26 and June 5 in 40 states and the District of Columbia, finding 125 cases of excessive use of force.
Brian Castner of Amnesty International said that they had seen situations where tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40-millimeter impact grenades were used inappropriately, mostly as combinations of low-lethal weapons.
A significant number of people still hold that having choices is important for avoiding gun use.
Corey Pegues of the NYPD’s former Deputy Inspector stated that “we should provide our officers with all manner of personal protective equipment rather than lethal weapons such as guns because police in America is the only profession where citizens have the power to take human life.”
There is a new tool called Bola Wrap, a handheld device that launches an 8-foot Kevlar rope to wrap around a suspect.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic devices (also known as sonic cannons), directed energy weapons that heat up human skin with laser beams, and new kinds of artillery shells made from materials such as foams and chalk dust, as well as stink bombs and electric stun guns.
Within days of George Floyd’s death at the hands of police, protests turned to violence, and Axon, the maker of electro-shock weapons, saw its stock price rise more than 18%, reaching an all-time high.
The market for low-kill weapons is expected to reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch videos to learn about new high-tech low-lethal tools used by police and how they can be regulated; what risks these tools pose for future law enforcement and community safety.
The UN says there are thousands of Pakistani insurgent fighters based in Afghanistan.
Islamabad – The UN report says that more than 6,000 Pakistani insurgents hide in Afghanistan, most of them illegal members of the so-called Pakistan Taliban, responsible for attacks on military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to the report released this week, the group known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) is linked to the Afghan branch of the Islamic State (IS).
Some members of the TTP even joined an organization under IS that is based in eastern Afghanistan.
Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to AP’s request for comment on these related issues.
It is reported that IS (also known as the Khorasan Province) in Afghanistan has been severely hit by Afghan security forces and US/NATO troops, sometimes even by attacks from the Taliban.
This report was prepared by the Analysis Support and Sanctions Monitoring Group, which is responsible for tracking terrorist organizations worldwide.
The report estimates that there are 2,200 members of the IS group in Afghanistan, despite its leadership being weakened; it still regards Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani as one of its leaders.
The report also notes that members of the monitoring team reportedly state that two senior ISIS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report states that while IS territory is shrinking, they still have the capacity to carry out attacks of varying scale in all regions of the country including Kabul.
The organization also seeks to co-opt Taliban fighters opposed to signing an agreement with the US, which was reached in February between the US and the Taliban.
The purpose of this agreement is for the US to end its 19-year war in Afghanistan and for the Taliban to guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is expected to also ensure that the Taliban fully commit themselves to fighting IS.
The second part of the agreement – possibly its most important section – calls for Taliban and Kabul political leaders to meet.
On Saturday afternoon, US State Department issued a statement saying that its envoy for peace Zalmay Khalilzad continues his busy efforts to push forward negotiations over prisoner release plans that have been repeatedly delayed owing to disagreements between both sides.
This US-backed agreement demands that the Afghan government release 5,000 Taliban prisoners in exchange for the release of 1,000 government and military personnel from the other side as part of the olive branch thrown out before negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government refuses to release about 600 Taliban prisoners and calls them high-profile criminals, but proposes to release only the remaining ones.
The Taliban rejected the government’s proposal.
“This is perhaps the best opportunity in history for both sides to engage in internal negotiations and a critical step toward ending four decades of war in Afghanistan,” the State Department said.
“Although significant progress has been made in exchange of prisoners, further efforts will be needed to solve the problem.”
Last week, Taliban spokesman said that the group planned to hold talks with political leaders in Kabul after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) ended on the last day of this month, provided it first completed prisoner releases.
The most worrying issue for Pakistan is the armed elements in Afghanistan, especially those linked to groups such as TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, Lashkar-e-Islam or the Balochistan Liberation Army; they carried out large-scale attacks on southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan provinces this month.
Multiple Pakistani soldiers were killed in combat with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
The TP organization carried out an extremely deadly attack on Pakistan in 2014 that resulted in the deaths of 140 members of the Pakistani Army Academy.
Most of them are students, some as young as five years old.
The report states that “foreign terrorist groups operating in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of two countries, with an estimated number of between 6,000 and 6,500 people, most of whom are members of the TTP.”
The world's largest bank HSBC denies it set traps for Chinese tech firm Huawei in U.S. investigations – RT Business News
The multinational bank HSBC based in London said on social media that it had not fabricated evidence against Huawei as was reported in Chinese media, leading to the arrest of Huawei’s chief financial officer in Canada.
A now-irrelevant WeChat article states that banks said their actions had not triggered the US to investigate this global telecommunications equipment supplier for allegedly violating US sanctions against Iran because an investigation into whether Huawei was in violation of US sanctions had already begun before the bank’s intervention in late 2016.
The bank said on Saturday that HSBC “has no hostile intent toward Huawei and there is no ‘collusion’ between them,” adding that it will never distort any facts relating to its customers.
In response to the US Department of Justice’s request for information, HSBC merely stated objective facts.
HSBC neither “fabricated” evidence nor “covered up” facts.
The platform has blocked this article and is warning it as “in violation of regulations.” The article content cannot be seen now.
After multiple Chinese media outlets (including those with government ties) accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up a trap for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this explanation.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei’s chief financial officer Meng Wanzhou on behalf of US authorities; since then, the company has been at the center of diplomatic news for over two years.
Ms. Meng has been charged with fraud for allegedly using shell companies to circumvent sanctions against Iran and concealing Huawei’s relationship with the Iranian company Skycom from banks.
The new evidence cited in The Global Times shows that HSBC was very aware of the relationship between this Chinese company and Skycom.
It is reported that it chose to cooperate with US authorities in order to avoid more severe penalties imposed on it in 2012, when HSBC was forced to admit to money laundering and sanctions violations, including those against Iran.
The bank was ordered to pay approximately $1.9 billion in fines and was required to take steps to prevent similar improper behavior.
The paper in question states that “there is evidence to suggest that HSBC knew about this relationship and deliberately sold its customers’ confidential business documents to the US authorities with the aim of allowing the US government to overlook the bank’s own improper conduct and avoid the penalties it should have been subject to under the 2012 Deferred Prosecution Agreement.”
This newspaper, under state control in China, adds that if it is confirmed that banks have engaged in “collusion,” they may face “severe penalties” in China (one of HSBC’s main markets).
This well-known bank has been subject to repeated criticism over recent weeks.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting implementation of the new National Security Law in Hong Kong, where it is headquartered and contributes half of its profits.
HSBC finds itself in an awkward position between two camps: US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “kowtowing,” while China’s media says it is too late to support.
Angola: Pedale Hospital Transferred to Ministry of Health Oversight
LUSAKA – President of the Republic João Lourenço decided on Friday to hand over oversight of construction of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital in Luanda to the Ministry of Health.
The statement released by the Office of the President of the Republic of China states that such a move is aimed at implementing government construction principles and expanding hospital networks so as to ensure better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also notes that “in this current special situation, these measures have enabled Angolan people to have access to a differentiated and valuable hospital with modern technology for diagnosing and treating complex diseases.”
The president’s decision is also informed by Angola’s population growth, which has fueled “a massive demand” for the most diverse and high-quality health services.
The need for such resources is reasonable, as hospitals encompass the treatment of complex diseases related to ophthalmology and maternal and child health, which should be within the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama -- Algeria lost a patriot and a steadfast fighter.
Algeria – President Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed condolences to National Assembly member Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama who passed away on Friday and said Algeria “lost an patriot and a fighter for the cause of the people and the country.”
Tebboutine wrote in his condolence letter that “Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has departed; Algeria lost an patriot who had devoted himself to the cause of the people and the country from the moment he picked up arms, never wavering in his resolve, steadfast in his determination, and fighting alongside the valiant mujahideen brothers until he took his last breath on this day we mourn with such sorrow, and can only listen to God’s will.”
“Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, was born to an extraordinary generation that were believers and patriots willing to die for their country.”
In 1960, they joined Wilaya VI's Tahar training camp at their life’s peak and became part of the revolution's ranks, freeing Algeria from the grip of vile colonialism and giving up their lives.
This deceased leader possessed an indomitable spirit and devoted himself to building a united and prosperous Algeria. His name is also well-known.
The President added: “In the distant south, Mahmoud Guemama is revered as a mediator who steps in to help his people reach reconciliation when the sparks of discord ignite.”
These qualities and values have earned Mahmoud Guemama respect and trust from the people of Tamannaret, elevating him to serve as a representative in the National People's Assembly for multiple terms; it is his ambassador to issues that concern the region’s people and their will, and also represents all Algerian people.
"There is no tribute that can praise Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama; may he rest in peace."
Today, he left us in his hour of greatest need in Algeria,” said the president.
Finally, Teboune said, "I pray to God Almighty that the departed may be bathed in your holy and compassionate light, and receive him with you among the shahid and sages in the boundless paradise."
I pray to God that He give him patience and comfort for his family and loved ones.... Only you have the supreme power.
Coronavirus Travel Guide: Countries Imposing Entry Restrictions on Bulgarian Passengers
More countries are implementing travel restrictions. The Czech Republic hopes to conduct coronavirus tests on Bulgarian seasonal workers starting this week. Denmark stipulates that Bulgarian citizens who have registered their residence in the country and hold employment contracts or business travel documents can enter.
Starting today, anyone who has visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must be quarantined in Italy under mandatory conditions. Finland only accepts travelers with “legitimate reasons” to enter.
Austria mandates that even if PCR test results are negative, mandatory quarantines must be followed. Germany has applied similar policies to those for incoming travelers. In the coming 14 days, one should not enter southern Romania without sufficient justification. Greece took more stringent measures at its land border.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that from Tuesday onwards, foreign passengers arriving in Greece from Bulgaria must present a negative PCR test certificate to Greek border authorities. The testing should be completed within 72 hours prior to arrival in Greece's southern neighboring country.
They also announced that travelers must have corresponding test certificates to be allowed entry; these documents must be issued in English and include three names and numbers from the passport or ID of the traveler. If they do not meet the above requirements, authorities in neighboring countries will not accept them.
Passengers must undergo nasopharyngeal swab testing at the extraction port/lab to complete an RT-PCR test in a laboratory. The accepted results include those obtained from tests conducted on samples taken from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been certified by relevant national accreditation bodies (not necessarily dedicated to testing for the novel coronavirus).
Greeks, Greek residents, and passengers with significant professional reasons are exempt from this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that travelers arriving in Greece on July 28 (Tuesday) and July 29 (Wednesday), who have not undergone testing prior to departure, will be tested upon arrival by the Greek authorities.
The following requirements remain in effect: Passengers traveling to Greece by air must complete an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) at least 24 hours before departure and obtain a QR code for it. The form should be submitted to Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
Currently, there is no change in requirements for travel between Bulgaria and Greece by land border crossing. The Foreign Ministry explains that “travellers crossing the land border into Greece regardless of nationality must have their electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) scanned at least two days before arrival.”
Closing Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda and Makaza checkpoints led to an increase in traffic flow at the border near Petrich. There were no issues with transit, nor did there exist any truck queues. These people were waiting to sail from Greece. Passengers had to wait about one hour and a half for their transit.
There are three checkpoints open along the Bulgarian-Greek border. Only the section between "Ilinden-Exohi" and Ivaylovgrad is closed at present.
Dominic Cummings visited SAS base and was held as "hostage"
Last week, Prime Minister and Chief Advisor tasted what it's like to be held hostage at an inspection visit to the base.
For some harassed civil servants, it may have an air of distinction to be held hostage by pointing a gun at Dominic Cummings’s head.
The stern prime minister’s chief advisor was there to taste the flavor of things when he visited the SAS headquarters in Stirling last week.
The Sunday Mail revealed that he had part of his Wednesday itinerary visiting an “inside anti-terror training facility,” where elite soldiers hone their skills in dealing with terrorists.
He was still being guided through the Hertford base to inspect the military equipment of that legion and listen to briefings on recent operations, including efforts to clear Iraq’s Islamic State terrorists.
The important visitors to Stirling’s lines were given special welcome – to experience “the indoor anti-terror training hall” where SAS trains up close combat.
Princess Diana had also visited here in 1983 during a training exercise when her hair was singed by the sparks from an accidentally thrown hand grenade.
It is known that Cummings arrived in helicopter and the base was named after the founder of the Special Air Service, Major General David Stirling.
He also visited the nearby Pons Ralis training area, which houses several other secret units.
This visit was one of several stops on his tour of Britain’s highly classified security facilities, which drew attention from political and military leaders alike.
When news of its plans was leaked earlier in the month, Conservative backbencher and chairman of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee, Tobias Ellwood, said that MPs should have the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
The Special Committee has rarely seen a situation in which politicians have appointed special advisors to give testimony.
It was understood that SAS officials used Wednesday's events to highlight the importance of maintaining Royal Marines and Airborne Brigades in numbers.
It is reported that these units, which have sent large numbers of personnel to special forces in the past, may be downsized or merged to cut costs.
A defense source said that SAS is made up of 250 soldiers, half of whom were previously paratroopers and special boat teams (SBS), whose size was smaller without support from the Royal Marines.
"Cummings was informed that he must preserve these units in their entirety."
Mr. Cummings has complained that military procurement is continuing to squander billions of pounds. He recently attended a meeting held at the headquarters of MI5 and MI6, and there is also a plan to visit the main SBS office in Porthleven, Cornwall, as well as the National Secret Laboratory in Broadweymouth, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of National Defense did not respond to the request for comment last night.
Sunday Morning Show: A Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
It is also the centennial of the first collaboration between Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart, one of Broadway's most legendary songwriting duos.
To honor these two songwriters, Broadway stars Jelani Alladin (of Frozen and Hercules) and Christiani Pitts (of The Jungle Book and Deep in the Heart of Texas), as well as dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (of My Fair Lady and Shuffle Along), performed together on the modern style – “The Lassie” based on Rodgers and Hammerstein’s original works, which was specially recorded for the audience of “Sunday Morning.”
Musical director and arranger for the performances is drummer Bryan Carter, with bassist Russell Hall on stage as well.
The California wildfires in Lassen County are under control.
Lassen County in northeastern Sacramento County, California, has recently experienced two wildfires that have burned an area exceeding 12,141 hectares; firefighting operations continue.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection said that as of Saturday morning, the "Gold" fire in the south of Adams County had been brought under control to just 40%, with many houses and pastures still at risk from the flames, which could have a major impact on local agriculture, livestock, and forestry.
The blaze has been burning for four days and has destroyed about 849.8 hectares of land and seven houses, injuring two firefighters who were sent to hospital.
Firefighters worked through the night to create and reinforce the firebreaks with limited resources.
But according to weather forecasts, it was windy and low-humidity that day.
The Hog Fire in Western Susanville has destroyed two buildings and about 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection said that after firefighters kept up night-and-day efforts to clear and reinforce fire lines, the blaze has been brought under control.
As of Saturday morning, the fire had been burning for six days and 45 percent of it was under control.
Be wary of those seemingly good horticultural tricks.
For plant scientists, this may be an unpleasant matter; but I am indeed deeply fascinated with horticultural tricks.
Thinking positively, I consider myself an open-minded or somewhat irrational optimist and that many things in the plant kingdom cannot be judged by intuition.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have fallen in this gardening trick countless times.
While I certainly believe in the value of trying new things, if not keen on opening “hell mode,” I can advise you to avoid some of my most common “trickster” gardening ploys.
First off, it could be the biggest red zone-- grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as revolutionary breakthrough; in fact, it is nothing more than an old trick from the 1920s that had been tried before.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family; their internal “pipes” are roughly similar, so grafting can indeed create an enhanced miracle.
But the problem is that if we create resource-intensive structures such as fruit and root crops, it requires a lot of energy to metabolize them, so plants that need to be grafted must simultaneously meet the energy needs of two different crops, which means that limited resources have to be diverted.
This means that these transplanted plants will yield very low yields and be quite different from what you see in your promotional images.
Moreover, crop maturity times vary, so tomatoes must be killed before potatoes can be harvested.
At the same time, propaganda images contain many deceptive tricks.
I have seen roses in some folders processed to look like they're all blue, and there were so many fruits on trees that I couldn't believe it.
Some merchants have even “borrowed” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim that they are trial sites on online shopping websites.
The actions of these few unscrupulous merchants require you to scrutinize such promotional photos with a suspicious eye – especially those from the Internet.
If it looks so good that it doesn’t look real, it probably is.
When it comes to blue roses, there is relatively little of it in plants, so if you see new varieties of the colorful haworthia, orchids, or cacti glowing with a bluish light under high-concentration “disinfectant,” they are almost certainly sprayed or dyed.
If suppliers explicitly state the situation, there’s not much to worry about, but they rarely do so.
Blue orchids revert to white, but cacti and haworthia coated with a photosensitivity dye will quickly die.
Finally, I must expose the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees and worse than bonsai seeds.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Bonsai is usually a mature shrub that has been pruned to form new growth directions with lines.
I know that I will continue to be fascinated by these horticultural tricks; this is one of the joys of experimenting.
But if you sidestep these issues, you may be able to get an item that matches what the seller has shown.
Spanish isolation measures: Do you need to be quarantined in Spain?
No need, but if you return from Spain after midnight tonight, you must quarantine yourself at home for 14 days.
Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said on Wednesday that they had decided not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because data showed an improvement in the spread of the virus.
But clearly, the latest data warrants concern and necessitates overturning our original decision.
We know that doing so will disappoint people, but we have always made it clear to everyone that we will closely monitor the epidemic situation in each country and if the virus were to reignite in one nation, we might ask them to be removed from our list of countries not implementing isolation measures altogether.
“Virus remains rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to suppress the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.”
The government has not yet announced regulations for British nationals traveling to Spain, but you should check your local regulations if you plan on going there.
Spanish Deputy Director of Emergency Health Services, Maria Jose Sierra, said that:
"We have a major outbreak here. This could be the second wave. We need to pay attention to what is happening in the coming weeks."
Pakistan team Imam-Ul-Haq injured during warm-up can still participate
Pakistan and England will play as opponents in three Test matches and three T20I games.
Imam-ul-Haq, a Pakistani cricket player who was injured to the left hand during an internal training match at Derby, is now able to compete.
The Pakistan Cricket Board stated in a statement that “this cricket player who is accustomed to using his left hand took part in a warm-up match before the start of the next game.”
On Day 1 of the match, when Imam from PCB White and Naseem Shah from PCB Green were hitting each other with their left hands, both were hit.
On Friday evening, Imam was injured when he was hit in the hand by a ball thrown by Shah and had to leave the field; however, he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the pitch on Day 3.
More than 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government department of the Tiruchirappalli municipal corporation in Tamil Nadu and one state-owned bank said that at least 38 employees of a main branch of the bank located in Tiruchirappalli had tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials said they had suggested that customers who had visited the branch voluntarily participate in a virus test.
Previously, an executive at this branch had other complications and died of COVID-19.
Officials say that they have conducted a large-scale medical screening of this branch recently and found these positive cases.
The branch manager said that they had completed disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officers are in contact with customers who have visited the bank.
Tamil Nadu is India's second-most-affected state in terms of confirmed cases, with Maharashtra having the highest number of infections as of Saturday. Tamil Nadu has reported a total of 206,737 confirmed cases so far.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built at a disputed location.
NEW DELHI (AP) -- A trust overseeing construction of Hindu temples in India said Prime Minister Narendra Modi is set to attend an inauguration ceremony for a temple under construction at a disputed site in northern India that was once home to a mosque built in the 16th century and razed to the ground by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is scheduled to take place on August 5 and organizers say that this day is auspicious for Hindus but also marks one year since the Indian Parliament revoked the semi-autonomous status of Jammu and Kashmir, which has a majority Muslim population.
Modi’s supporters and opponents in India’s Hindu nationalist Indian National Congress (INC) will not miss this symbolic day when the party has for decades pledged to wrest Kashmir’s autonomy and build a temple on the site of the original Mughal mosque that was demolished there, where devotees believe Ram was born.
The coronavirus continues to spread throughout India, and it is reported that the country has become the third-largest source of global infections; given this, the Indian nationalist religious group Vishwa Hindu Parishad (World Hindu Conference) called for the ceremony to be broadcast live on national television with restrictions on the number of attendees and viewers.
The organization’s spokesman Vinod Bansal said in an interview with reporters on Saturday that the temple would be “an eternal centre of social harmony, ethnic unity and cultural integration that will awaken Hindu characteristics.”
The dispute over the place, which had lasted a century, was resolved after the Indian People’s Party won an overwhelming victory in elections last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of temple trusts, saying it would allocate five acres elsewhere to Muslim petitioners.
For decades, fervent Hindu nationalists have claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the ancient temple in Ayodhya.
The riots that followed the destruction of the mosque in December 1992 were sparked by community violence and resulted in the deaths of approximately 2,000 people, most of whom were Muslims.
Meanwhile, the Special Court is still trying cases of mosque demolition.
An architect from Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India, proposed building a sandstone tower-shaped temple with five domes at a height of 49 meters (161 feet).
